+<h2>Model</h2>
+<ul>
+<li><code>build_1d_cnn(input_shape, num_classes)</code> - compiled Keras Sequential</li>
+<li><code>python model.py</code> - prints the layer summary</li>
+</ul>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Feature extractor</th><td>3 x (Conv1D kernel 3 + ReLU -&gt; MaxPooling1D 2), 32 -&gt; 64 -&gt; 128 filters; learns its own band-pass banks instead of an FFT</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Classifier head</th><td>Flatten -&gt; Dense(128) + ReLU -&gt; Dropout(0.5) -&gt; Dense(num_classes) + softmax</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Compile</th><td>Adam, categorical_crossentropy; 2,096,068 parameters for (1024, 1) input and 4 classes</td></tr>
+</table>
+<h2>Advanced concepts</h2>
+<p class="note">The ideas this model actually leans on, each stated with its failure mode.</p>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Receptive field arithmetic</h3>
+<p>Three kernel-3 convolutions separated by max-pool 2 give a receptive field of 1 + 2 + 4 + ... samples of context per output activation. Widening the kernel or adding a dilation is the only way to see a full shaft revolution without a deeper stack - the reason the upstream model stops at 1024 samples.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Convolution beats handcrafted spectra</h3>
+<p>Classical bearing diagnostics hand you envelope spectra and defect frequencies (BPFO/BPFI). A 1D-CNN learns its own filter bank end to end, which is why it transfers across machines - and why it needs thousands of labelled windows to beat the handcrafted baseline at all.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Surrogate-data honesty</h3>
+<p>The platform has 10-minute RMS SCADA, not 20 kHz DAQ. Waveforms are synthesised and carry vibration_is_surrogate=True, so every metric this model reports is a property of the pipeline, not of a real bearing. Swapping in a real DAQ path changes only the adapter's input view, never the network.</p>
+</div>
